"""Open-question versions of the former multiple-choice catalog questions.

These questions are asked aloud, so they carry no options. OPEN_TITLES rewrites
the prompts that only made sense with the options in view. OPEN_ANSWERS replaces
model answers that were too short to compare a spoken answer against.
Keys are the question id without its bank prefix.
"""

OPEN_TITLES = {
    "cml-scaling": "Your features range from 0–1 (a ratio) to 0–1,000,000 (income). Which kinds of model need the features scaled, and which do not?",
    "qa-bi-encoder-precompute": "Why can a bi-encoder compute document representations once at indexing time, while a cross-encoder cannot?",
    "qa-graphrag-when": "What kind of question does plain chunk-based RAG fail on, where GraphRAG helps?",
    "qa-topology-debug": "You're building a multi-agent system that has to be easy to debug when something goes wrong. Which topology do you choose, and why?",
    "rag-ft-long-context": "A 300-page policy manual is updated monthly. Answers must cite the exact clause, and the bot should write in your brand's voice. What combination of retrieval, long context and fine-tuning fits?",
    "rag-kg-vs-vector": "What kind of query needs a knowledge graph rather than a vector index?",
    "rag-graphrag-global": "What kind of question needs GraphRAG's global search rather than local search?",
    "rag-dont-cache": "In a RAG system, when should you not cache answers?",
}

OPEN_ANSWERS = {
    "cml-high-variance-tree": "A 28-point gap between training and validation is the textbook sign of high variance: the tree has memorised the training set, down to its noise. The first thing to try is constraining it with a maximum depth, a minimum number of samples per leaf, or cost-complexity pruning, choosing the setting by cross-validation. The next step is to average many trees in a random forest, or use gradient boosting with shallow trees, which cuts variance further. More data also helps if you can get it. Training longer or adding features would make the gap worse.",
    "cml-high-bias": "Both numbers are low and close together, and people reach 95%, so the model is underfitting: high bias. It cannot fit even the data it has seen, which means the problem is capacity or optimisation, not generalisation. Add capacity with a bigger or better-suited model, for example a convolutional network or a pretrained backbone, improve the features, reduce regularisation, and train longer with a tuned learning rate. Collecting more data is the classic wrong move here, because a model that cannot fit the current data will not fit more of it. Human performance gives the target to aim for.",
    "cml-time-series-split": "The validation scheme leaked the future. Random folds put neighbouring days, which are highly correlated, on both sides of the split, so the model was tested on days it had effectively already seen and trained on days after the ones it was tested on. Validation has to mimic deployment. A forecaster always predicts the future from the past, so train on everything before a cut-off date and test on what follows, using a rolling or expanding window over several cut-offs. Also check that lag and rolling features only use values available at prediction time. The honest score will be lower, and closer to production.",
    "cml-leaky-feature": "I conclude the feature is leaking the label, not that the model is good. For every feature, ask whether you would know its value at prediction time. A closure reason is written when the account closes, which is the event being predicted, so the model is reading the answer. Remove it and anything derived from it, then rebuild features as of a point in time before the prediction date. Expect validation AUC to fall to something believable. An AUC of 0.99 on a churn problem, with one dominant feature, should always trigger this check before anyone celebrates.",
    "cml-smote-before-split": "The test set is contaminated. SMOTE creates new minority points by interpolating between real ones and their neighbours. Done before splitting, synthetic points built from a test example land in training, and points built from training examples land in test, so the model gets credit for recognising near-copies. The recall figure is inflated and will not hold in production. Split first, then resample only the training portion, ideally inside a pipeline so cross-validation repeats it within each fold. The test set must keep the real class balance, because that is what the model will face. Class weights are often a simpler alternative to resampling.",
    "cml-accuracy-imbalance": "With 1% fraud, a model that flags nobody also scores 99% accuracy, so accuracy mostly measures the majority class. Report precision and recall at the operating threshold, with the confusion matrix in real counts, so the business sees how many frauds are caught and how many honest users are flagged. Use PR-AUC to compare models, because it is dominated by performance on the rare class. ROC-AUC counts true negatives in its false-positive rate, and with 99 negatives per positive that rate stays small even when most flags are wrong. If costs are known, report expected cost per thousand users as well.",
    "cml-threshold-capacity": "When a person acts on each flag, the operational constraint sets the threshold. With capacity for 200 reviews a day, rank the day's cases by score and send the top 200, which is a top-k rule and not a fixed probability cut-off. Evaluate the model with precision at k and recall at k, because that is the quantity the team experiences. Check the score distribution over time, since a fixed threshold would send too many or too few cases as volumes shift. If the cost of missed fraud is known, compare it with the cost of a review to decide whether the team should grow.",
    "cml-calibration": "AUC measures ranking only: a model can order customers perfectly and still say 0.9 where the true rate is 0.3. Pricing uses the probability itself, so check calibration. Plot a reliability diagram by bucketing the predictions and comparing the average prediction with the observed rate in each bucket, and compute the Brier score or expected calibration error. If the curve is off, fit Platt scaling or isotonic regression on held-out data, never on the training set. Boosted trees and naive Bayes are often poorly calibrated out of the box. Check calibration within important segments too, because a model can be calibrated overall and wrong for one group.",
    "cml-l1-sparse": "L1 regularisation, the lasso. The L1 penalty's constraint region has corners on the axes, so the optimum often lands where many weights are exactly zero, which gives feature selection as part of training. L2 only shrinks weights towards zero and keeps all 500. Choose the penalty strength by cross-validation and standardise the features first, because the penalty depends on scale. Use elastic net, which mixes L1 and L2, when the important features are correlated with each other: plain lasso tends to pick one of a correlated group arbitrarily, and the choice can change between samples of the data.",
    "cml-scaling": "Tree-based models do not care: decision trees, random forests and gradient-boosted trees. Each split only compares values within one feature against a threshold, so any monotonic rescaling leaves the splits unchanged. Anything based on distances, dot products, gradient descent or penalties on weights does need standardised features: k-nearest neighbours, k-means, SVMs, PCA, regularised linear and logistic regression, and neural networks. Without scaling, income would dominate every distance and the penalty would treat the two weights unfairly. Fit the scaler on the training set only and apply it to validation and test, otherwise the scaling itself leaks information.",
    "cml-impurity-importance": "Not without checking. Mean decrease in impurity is computed on training data and is biased towards high-cardinality and continuous features, because a feature with 30,000 values offers many more possible splits and some will look good by chance. Permutation importance on held-out data asks a cleaner question: how much worse does validation get when this feature is shuffled? If the importance vanishes there, the forest was memorising. If zip code still matters, encode it sensibly, for example target encoding computed within folds, or grouping to region. Also remember that correlated features share importance, so a low rank does not prove a feature is useless.",
    "cml-knn-dimensions": "This is the curse of dimensionality. k-nearest neighbours treats every feature equally in its distance, so each irrelevant feature adds noise to every distance and the five useful ones are drowned out. In high dimensions the nearest and farthest neighbours also end up at nearly the same distance, so the word nearest stops meaning much. The fixes are to select features, reduce dimensions with PCA or a learned embedding, or learn a metric that weights features. Otherwise switch to a model that decides for itself which features matter, such as gradient-boosted trees or a regularised linear model.",
    "cml-collinearity": "This is multicollinearity. When two features carry nearly the same information, many combinations of their weights fit the data equally well, for example a large positive weight on one and a large negative weight on the other. Small changes in the data then swing the coefficients, even though the predictions stay stable. If you need interpretable coefficients, drop one feature, combine them, or use ridge regression, which spreads the weight across the pair stably. The variance inflation factor measures the problem directly, and values above about 10 are a common warning. If you only need predictions, collinearity matters much less.",
    "cml-missing-informative": "The data is not missing at random: not reporting income tells you something about the applicant. So do not drop those rows, which would discard 30% of the data and the signal with it. Impute a value, the median or a model-based estimate, so the model has a number, and add a binary indicator so it can learn that missingness itself predicts default. Gradient-boosted trees such as XGBoost and LightGBM handle missing values natively by learning which branch they go down. Fit the imputation on training data only. Also ask why income is missing, because a change in the application form could shift this pattern later.",
    "dl-flat-loss": "A loss of 2.30 is the natural log of 10, so the model is predicting a uniform distribution over the ten classes and nothing is being learned. My first step is to try to overfit a single small batch. If the loss will not go to zero on eight examples, the code is broken, not the problem hard. Then I check the classic causes: a learning rate of zero or far too small, an optimiser that was not given the model's parameters, a missing optimizer.step() or a misplaced zero_grad(), frozen layers, a detached graph, or inputs and labels that do not line up. Printing gradient norms per layer usually points at the fault quickly.",
    "dl-val-rises": "The model is overfitting. After epoch 5 it keeps improving on the training set by fitting noise specific to it, which no longer helps on unseen data. The immediate action is early stopping: keep the checkpoint with the best validation loss. Then reduce the gap with regularisation, such as weight decay, dropout, data augmentation or label smoothing, with a smaller model, or with more data if it is available. It is worth checking the validation metric as well as the loss, because accuracy can keep improving while the loss rises as the model becomes overconfident on a few examples.",
    "dl-oscillating": "Wild oscillation with no downward trend is the classic sign of a learning rate that is too high: each step overshoots the minimum. I would lower it by a factor of three to ten first, and add warmup if there is none, since early steps with a cold optimiser are the most unstable. I would also check that the batch is not tiny, because very noisy gradients look similar, and add gradient clipping. A learning-rate range test, increasing the rate gradually over a few hundred steps and watching where the loss starts to blow up, finds a sensible value quickly. If the loss still jumps, I look for bad or mislabelled batches.",
    "dl-nan-fp16": "Overflow is the most likely cause. fp16 has a narrow range, with a maximum near 65,504, so a spike in activations, logits or gradients overflows to infinity, and the next operation, such as infinity minus infinity or zero times infinity, produces NaN. Fixes, roughly in order: use bf16 if the hardware supports it, since it keeps fp32's exponent range; use automatic mixed precision with dynamic loss scaling for fp16; clip gradients; lower the learning rate; and look for an unstable operation such as a softmax, log or division on unclamped values. Anomaly detection or logging the largest activation per layer shows where the first infinity appears.",
    "dl-eval-mode": "I forgot to put the model in evaluation mode. In training mode, dropout randomly zeroes activations and batch norm uses the current batch's statistics and keeps updating its running averages, so outputs change from run to run and depend on how the data is batched. Call model.eval() before evaluation, wrap the loop in torch.no_grad() or inference_mode to save memory and time, and call model.train() before training resumes. If the numbers still differ after that, the remaining suspects are random augmentation applied to the validation set, shuffling combined with dropping the last batch, or non-deterministic GPU kernels.",
    "dl-adamw": "Because in Adam an L2 penalty does not behave like weight decay. The L2 term is added to the gradient, and Adam then divides the gradient by its running estimate of the gradient's magnitude. Parameters with large gradients therefore get less regularisation and those with small gradients get more, which nobody intended. AdamW decouples the two: it subtracts the learning rate times the weight decay times the weight separately from the adaptive update, so every weight decays at the same relative rate. In practice it generalises better and its settings transfer more reliably between models. Bias and normalisation parameters are usually excluded from decay.",
    "dl-batch-lr": "The linear scaling rule: multiply the learning rate by the batch-size ratio, so four times here, and warm up over the first few epochs. The reasoning is that a batch four times larger gives a gradient with less noise, and you take a quarter as many steps per epoch, so each step can be larger. The rule holds for SGD up to fairly large batches and then breaks down. For Adam-style optimisers, square-root scaling, twice the rate here, is a common heuristic. Either way it is a starting point, and I would confirm it with a short sweep around the predicted value.",
    "dl-grad-clip": "Gradient-norm clipping. Occasional loss spikes usually come from a rare batch that produces a huge gradient, and one oversized step throws the weights off, after which the model slowly recovers. Clipping rescales the whole gradient when its norm exceeds a threshold, commonly 1.0, so the direction is kept but the step is bounded. Log the gradient norm so you can set the threshold just above its typical value. If spikes continue, lower the learning rate or lengthen warmup, inspect the batches that cause them for corrupt or extreme examples, and look at Adam's epsilon and beta2, since a very small second-moment estimate can also inflate a step.",
    "dl-batchnorm-small": "Batch norm normalises each channel with the mean and variance of the current batch. With two images those estimates are mostly noise, so every step sees differently normalised activations, and the running statistics used at evaluation time do not match what training saw. The fix is a normalisation that does not depend on batch size: GroupNorm or LayerNorm compute statistics within each example. If you want to keep batch norm, SyncBatchNorm computes statistics across all GPUs, which raises the effective batch. Freezing the batch-norm layers of a pretrained backbone is another common choice. Gradient accumulation does not help, because the statistics are still per micro-batch.",
    "dl-residual": "Residual connections. Worse training accuracy with more layers is the degradation problem, not overfitting, because an overfit model would do better on the training set. In principle the deeper network could copy the shallow one and set the extra layers to the identity, but plain layers find the identity hard to learn and gradients weaken as they pass through many of them. A residual block computes x plus f(x), so the identity is the default and gradients have a direct path back. Normalisation and careful initialisation help too. This is the ResNet result, and it is why every transformer block has two residual connections.",
    "qa-bpe-rare-word": "It splits the word into several subword pieces it already knows, something like un, fath, om, ability, and never emits an unknown token. BPE builds its vocabulary by starting from individual characters or bytes and repeatedly merging the most frequent adjacent pair into a new token until it reaches a target size. At encoding time it applies those merges in the order they were learned. Frequent words end up as one token, and rare words fall back to smaller pieces, down to single bytes if needed. The practical effects are that rare words cost more tokens and that the model has to compose their meaning from the parts.",
    "qa-positional-encoding": "It cannot tell them apart. Self-attention on its own is permutation-equivariant: each token's output depends on which other tokens are present, not on where they are. Without positional information, the two sentences are the same set of tokens, so each token gets the same representation in both and the model behaves like a bag of words. Positional encoding injects order into the input, either by adding sinusoidal or learned position vectors to the embeddings, or with relative schemes such as rotary embeddings that act inside attention. A decoder with a causal mask can recover some order from the mask alone, but an encoder cannot.",
    "qa-sqrt-dk": "The softmax saturates and gradients vanish. If the query and key components have roughly unit variance, their dot product has variance of about d_k, so with 128 dimensions the scores have a standard deviation near 11. Softmax over values that far apart is close to one-hot: one key gets almost all the weight. In that region the gradient of the softmax is near zero, so the attention weights stop learning, and training is slow or unstable from the first steps. Dividing by the square root of d_k brings the variance back to about one, which keeps softmax in a range where gradients flow.",
    "qa-cross-attention": "The queries come from the decoder: the representations of the target tokens generated so far, after the decoder's own self-attention. The keys and values come from the encoder's final output, the encoded source sentence. So each target position asks which source positions matter for the next word, and pulls in a weighted mix of their values. That contrasts with self-attention, where queries, keys and values all come from the same sequence. The encoder output is computed once and reused at every decoding step, so its keys and values can be cached. There is no causal mask in cross-attention, only a padding mask on the source.",
    "qa-causal-mask": "Generation falls apart. Without the causal mask, each position can attend to later positions during training, so predicting token n the model simply looks at token n itself in the input, which sits one place ahead. It learns to copy, and the loss drops very fast for that reason. At generation time the future tokens do not exist, so the copying shortcut has nothing to read and the model produces repetitive or meaningless text. The causal mask blocks attention to later positions, which makes training match inference. A training loss that falls unusually quickly towards zero is the warning sign for this kind of leak.",
    "ft-rag-or-finetune": "RAG. The rule of thumb is retrieval for knowledge and fine-tuning for behaviour. A manual that changes weekly would need retraining every week, and a fine-tuned model still could not show where an answer came from, or reliably forget last week's policy. With retrieval you re-index the changed pages, the answer is grounded in the current text, and it can cite the clause. Fine-tuning is good at teaching a format, a tone, a task-specific skill, or shortening prompts, and much less reliable at teaching facts. Many production systems use both: a lightly fine-tuned model that is good at answering from retrieved documents.",
    "ft-lora-what": "LoRA freezes the pretrained weights and trains a pair of small matrices beside chosen weight matrices, usually the attention projections. For a weight matrix W of size d by d, it learns B of size d by r and A of size r by d, with r much smaller than d, and uses W plus alpha over r times BA in the forward pass. B starts at zero, so training begins from the original model. Only A and B get gradients and optimiser state, so memory and checkpoint size drop sharply, often to well under 1% of the parameters. The adapter can be merged into W after training, so inference costs nothing extra, or kept separate and swapped per customer.",
    "ft-qlora": "QLoRA adds quantisation of the frozen base model, so fine-tuning fits in far less memory. It stores the base weights in 4-bit NormalFloat, a data type designed for normally distributed weights, with double quantisation to compress the quantisation constants as well. In the forward pass the weights are dequantised on the fly, and gradients flow only into LoRA adapters kept in bf16. Paged optimisers handle memory spikes. A 7B model's base weights take about 3.5 GB instead of 14 GB, so fine-tuning fits on one consumer GPU. The costs are slower training steps and a small quality risk, which the adapters largely recover.",
    "ft-forgetting": "This is catastrophic forgetting. Training only on one narrow distribution moves the weights towards it and overwrites general behaviour that the fine-tuning data never exercises. Mitigations: replay, which means mixing in roughly 10 to 30% general instruction data; a smaller learning rate and fewer epochs; and parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA, which change fewer weights and can be dialled down or removed. Just as important is an evaluation suite that includes general tasks alongside the legal ones, so the regression shows up before shipping. If legal knowledge is the real need, retrieval may deliver it without changing the model at all.",
    "ft-loss-vs-quality": "Validation loss and answer quality measure different things. The loss uses teacher forcing: the model always sees the correct previous tokens and is scored on the next one. At inference it builds on its own outputs, and small errors compound. Lower loss can also come from learning the reference answers' phrasing and length, or from overfitting to a narrow style, without getting better at the task. So evaluate generated outputs on held-out prompts with task metrics, human review, or a judge model you have validated against people, and choose checkpoints by that. Check the decoding settings too, since a served temperature can differ from the notebook's.",
    "ft-chat-template": "The chat template. Chat models learn a precise format: role markers, special tokens, and an end-of-turn token. If the server formats the conversation differently from training, even by a missing end-of-turn token, a doubled start token or a different system-prompt marker, the model sees an unfamiliar format and does not know where its turn ends, so it rambles on. Use the tokenizer's own chat template in both training and serving, and make sure the end-of-turn token is set as a stop token. To confirm, send a training prompt to the served endpoint and compare the exact token ids with the notebook's.",
    "ft-dpo": "Both learn from preference pairs, a preferred and a rejected answer to the same prompt. RLHF with PPO first trains a reward model on those pairs, then optimises the policy with reinforcement learning against it, with a KL penalty to stay near the reference model. That means sampling, a second model and many sensitive hyperparameters. DPO shows the same KL-constrained objective has a closed-form solution, so it can be optimised directly with a simple classification-style loss that raises the likelihood of the preferred answer relative to the rejected one, measured against the frozen reference model. It needs no reward model and no sampling loop, which makes it simpler and more stable.",
    "ft-data-quality": "The 1,000 expert examples, usually. For instruction-following style, the model already has the knowledge from pre-training, and fine-tuning mostly teaches the format and the manners. A small set of consistent, high-quality examples does that well, while a large noisy set teaches inconsistency, errors and whatever habits the scraped answers have. A model imitates its training data faithfully, including the bad parts. The larger set is still useful if you filter it hard, for example by scoring and keeping the best few thousand. For teaching a genuinely new skill or domain you need more data, and quality still matters.",
    "srv-decode-bound": "Memory bandwidth, not raw compute. Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel with big matrix multiplications, which is compute-bound. Decode produces one token per forward pass, and for each token the GPU has to read every weight from memory and use it for only a few operations, so the speed limit is how fast weights can be streamed. Tokens per second is roughly bandwidth divided by model size in bytes. That is why quantisation speeds up decode, and why batching many users together is far more efficient: the weights are read once per step and used for every sequence in the batch.",
    "srv-gqa": "To shrink the KV cache without losing much quality. In decode, every step reads the whole cache of keys and values for all earlier tokens, and with full multi-head attention each head stores its own. GQA lets groups of query heads share one key-value head, so the cache and the memory bandwidth it needs shrink by the group factor, often four or eight times. That means longer contexts and more concurrent sequences on the same GPU. Multi-query attention, with a single key-value head, is the extreme case and costs more quality. GQA sits between the two and keeps quality close to full multi-head attention.",
    "srv-continuous-batching": "It schedules at every decode step. Output lengths vary widely, and in static batching a request that finishes early holds an empty slot until the longest one ends, while new requests wait for the whole batch. Continuous, or in-flight, batching evicts finished sequences and inserts waiting ones straight away, so the batch stays full. Throughput rises and queueing time falls. Combined with paged KV caches, which let sequences of different lengths share memory without fragmentation, it is the main reason modern serving engines such as vLLM get several times more throughput from the same GPU. The trade-off is that per-token latency varies with the current load.",
    "srv-ttft": "Time to first token is the bad metric, while time per output token is fine. TTFT covers queueing plus prefill of the whole prompt, so long system prompts and retrieved context make it slow. The usual fixes are prefix caching, which keeps the KV cache of a shared system prompt and reuses it; trimming or reranking retrieved context so the prompt is shorter; chunked prefill, so one long prompt does not block everyone else; and enough capacity that requests do not queue. Report TTFT and time per output token separately, at p50 and p99, because they have different causes and different fixes.",
    "srv-prefix-cache": "Prefix caching, sometimes called prompt caching. Running prefill for 3,000 identical tokens on every request is pure waste. The server stores the KV cache blocks for a common prefix and maps new requests onto them, so only the new part of the prompt is computed, which cuts time to first token and compute. Many API providers bill cached input tokens at a discount for the same reason. The cache only matches an exact token prefix, so put the stable parts of the prompt first and the variable parts, such as the date or the user's name, last. One early changing token invalidates everything after it.",
    "srv-temp0": "Floating-point arithmetic that depends on batching. Greedy decoding picks the highest-probability token, but the logits themselves vary by tiny amounts depending on which other requests share the batch, because the kernel chosen and the order of additions change with batch shape. When two tokens are nearly tied, that is enough to flip the choice, and the rest of the text diverges from there. Different GPU types or replicas add the same kind of variation. Batch-invariant kernels can make serving fully deterministic at some cost in speed. If reproducibility matters, cache responses or fix the seed and hardware, and do not treat temperature 0 as a guarantee.",
    "dl-oom-accumulate": "Use gradient accumulation. Activation memory scales with the micro-batch size, so run micro-batches of 8, divide each loss by 4, call backward on each so the gradients add up, and call optimizer.step() and zero_grad() once after the fourth. That gives the same update as a batch of 32, apart from batch-norm statistics, which still see 8 examples. If more room is needed, add mixed precision, which roughly halves activation memory, and activation checkpointing, which recomputes activations during the backward pass in exchange for extra compute. The cost of accumulation is only wall-clock time, since the same number of examples is processed.",
    "dl-bf16": "Because bf16 has the range of fp32, so it does not overflow. Both formats are 16 bits. fp16 spends its bits on precision, with 10 stored mantissa bits, and has a small range with a maximum of about 65,504. bf16 keeps fp32's 8-bit exponent, so its range matches fp32, and stores only 7 mantissa bits. Neural network training is far more sensitive to overflow and underflow than to low precision, so bf16 trains stably without the loss scaling that fp16 needs. Modern accelerators support it natively. Sensitive operations, such as the loss and some normalisation statistics, are usually still kept in fp32.",
    "dl-augment-split": "Split by original image before augmenting. Any group of near-duplicates, such as augmentations of one photo, frames from the same video or scans from the same patient, must stay on one side of the split, otherwise validation measures whether the model recognises pictures it has trained on. So split first, by group, then augment the training set only, ideally on the fly inside the data loader. Leave validation images unaugmented. Check for near-duplicates across splits with perceptual hashes or embedding similarity, because the source data may already contain them. After the fix, validation accuracy should drop towards the 81% seen on new photos.",
    "qa-rag-or-finetune": "Retrieve. A price list that changes every day cannot live in the weights: fine-tuning is slow and costly to repeat, the model cannot reliably forget yesterday's price, and it cannot show where a figure came from. With retrieval, the system looks up the relevant items at query time and puts them in the context, so an update is a re-index, not a retraining run. For prices specifically, an exact lookup by product id through a tool or database query is safer than similarity search over embeddings, because a nearly matching product with a different price is the worst kind of error. Fine-tuning can still help with tone or format.",
    "qa-chunk-size": "The chunks are too large. A 2,000-token chunk covers many clauses, so its single embedding is an average of all of them, and the specific clause barely moves it. Retrieval finds the right document but ranks chunks poorly within it, and the model then has to find one sentence in a long passage. Smaller chunks, split on the document's own structure such as clauses and sections, with a little overlap, fix the precision problem. Going too small has the opposite cost: a chunk loses the context needed to make sense of it. Retrieving small chunks and then expanding to the parent section gives both precision and context.",
    "qa-dense-sparse": "Hybrid search: add a sparse keyword retriever such as BM25 beside the dense one. Dense retrieval compares learned embedding vectors by meaning, so it catches paraphrases and synonyms, but an identifier like E-4012 has no meaning for the embedding model to hold on to and ends up near other error text. Sparse retrieval matches the literal term, so it is exact on codes, ids, product names and jargon, and blind to synonyms. Run both and merge the results, commonly with reciprocal rank fusion, then rerank the combined list. For pure identifier queries, an exact-match filter on a metadata field is even more reliable.",
    "qa-why-rerank-shortlist": "Cost. A cross-encoder reads the query and one document together in a single forward pass, which is why it is accurate, but it also means nothing about a document can be computed before the query arrives. Scoring 10 million documents would take 10 million forward passes per query, which is hours of GPU time. A bi-encoder embeds documents once at indexing time, so a query needs one embedding and an approximate nearest-neighbour search that takes milliseconds. The standard design uses the cheap method to cut the corpus to about a hundred candidates and spends the expensive model only on those.",
    "qa-bi-encoder-precompute": "A bi-encoder encodes the query and each document independently into vectors and compares them with a cheap similarity function such as a dot product. Because a document's vector does not depend on the query, it can be computed once at indexing time and stored in a vector index, and a search then costs one query embedding plus a nearest-neighbour lookup. A cross-encoder feeds the query and document through one model together, with full attention between them, so its output only exists once both are known and nothing can be precomputed. It is much more accurate and much slower, so it is used only to rerank a shortlist.",
    "qa-graphrag-when": "Multi-hop and whole-corpus questions. Chunk-based RAG finds passages similar to the query, so it fails when the answer needs facts chained across documents that are not similar to each other, for example which suppliers of our largest customer were affected by the recall, or when it asks about the collection as a whole, such as the main themes across all reports. GraphRAG builds a knowledge graph of entities and relationships from the documents and retrieves by traversing it, alongside or in place of chunk similarity, and it can summarise communities of related entities. The costs are an expensive indexing step and a graph that has to be kept up to date.",
    "qa-faithfulness": "Faithfulness, also called groundedness. It asks whether every claim in the answer is supported by the retrieved context. The usual method splits the answer into individual claims and checks each one against the chunks with an entailment model or a judge model, then reports the supported fraction. This answer would score below one because of the unsupported claim. It is different from answer relevance, which asks whether the answer addresses the question, and from context precision and recall, which judge the retrieval. A citation next to a sentence proves nothing by itself, so the check has to compare the claim with the cited text.",
    "qa-topk-hurts": "The extra chunks are mostly noise. Going from 5 to 30 adds a few useful passages and many irrelevant or redundant ones, and the model's attention on the chunks that answer the question is diluted. Long contexts also suffer from the lost-in-the-middle effect, where information away from the start and end is recalled less reliably, and distracting passages that look relevant can pull the answer the wrong way. The usual fix is to retrieve widely but pass on narrowly: fetch 30 or more candidates, rerank them with a cross-encoder, and give the model only the best few. Latency and cost fall as well.",
    "qa-crag": "Corrective RAG, usually shortened to CRAG. It inserts a grading step right after retrieval: a lightweight evaluator, a small model or an LLM call, scores whether the retrieved chunks are relevant and sufficient for the question. If they are, generation proceeds. If they are not, the system rewrites the query, searches more broadly, or falls back to another source such as web search, and only then answers. If the result is ambiguous it can combine both. The point is to avoid generating from context that was never going to support an answer. The costs are extra latency and a grader that needs its own evaluation.",
    "qa-adaptive-rag": "Adaptive RAG: route each query by complexity before deciding how to handle it. A small classifier or a cheap LLM call labels the query. A question the model can answer from its own knowledge skips retrieval, a straightforward lookup gets one retrieval pass, and a complex one is routed into decomposition or multi-step retrieval. Simple queries stay fast and cheap, and the expensive path is used only where it pays. The router is the weak point: a complex query sent down the simple path gets a poor answer, so measure routing accuracy on labelled queries and bias uncertain cases towards the more thorough path.",
    "qa-raptor": "A hierarchical summary tree, the RAPTOR approach. It clusters related chunks, summarises each cluster, then clusters and summarises those summaries again, going up several levels until a few top-level summaries cover the whole corpus. All levels are embedded and indexed together. A broad question about themes then matches a high-level summary, and a narrow question about one figure matches a leaf chunk, so retrieval is no longer stuck at one fixed granularity. The costs are many LLM calls at indexing time, summaries that can drop or distort details, and rebuilding parts of the tree when documents change.",
    "qa-cag-manual": "Skip retrieval and put the whole manual in the prompt. Forty pages is roughly 20,000 to 30,000 tokens, which fits easily in a 200k-token context. Place the manual at the start of the prompt and use prompt caching, so its KV cache is computed once and reused, which is sometimes called cache-augmented generation. The model then sees everything on every query, so there is no chunking, embedding, index or retrieval failure to debug, and a twice-yearly update is just a new cached prefix. This only works while the corpus is small and stable. Once it outgrows the context or changes often, retrieval becomes necessary again.",
    "qa-hyde": "HyDE, hypothetical document embeddings. A terse query and a long formal document sit far apart in embedding space even when they are about the same thing. So before searching, have the LLM write a hypothetical answer to the query, then embed that answer and search with it. The made-up answer may be wrong on the facts, but it is written in the vocabulary and style of the real documents, so it lands much closer to them. The costs are one extra LLM call per query and a risk that a confidently wrong draft steers retrieval off course, so many systems search with both the original query and the draft.",
    "qa-acl-where": "At retrieval time, as a filter inside the search itself. Each chunk carries access metadata, such as tenant, groups or document permissions, and the query applies a filter scoped to exactly what the requesting user is authorised to see, using the user's identity from the authenticated session and never from the prompt. Filtering the LLM's output afterwards is not enough, because by then the model has already read documents the user should not see and can leak them in paraphrase. Permissions also change, so the metadata must be kept in sync with the source system, and the same check must cover caches and any later step that fetches more documents.",
    "qa-ingestion-tables": "Ingestion, the PDF parsing step. Every later stage, chunking, embedding and retrieval, works on whatever text the parser produced, so a failure there silently corrupts everything after it. Plain text extraction reads a table in whatever order the characters are stored, which loses rows and columns and leaves prices beside the wrong products. I would open the extracted text for one bad page and compare it with the PDF. The fix is a layout-aware parser that detects tables and keeps their structure, output as Markdown or HTML, with each row or the whole table kept in one chunk along with its headers.",
    "qa-finetune-embeddings": "Fine-tune the embedding model on those pairs. A general model learned similarity from web text, so it treats passages on the same topic as close, while in law relevance depends on jurisdiction, the exact provision and how terms are defined. With 5,000 labelled query-passage pairs, train with a contrastive loss that pulls relevant pairs together and pushes others apart, and mine hard negatives: passages on the right topic that are not legally relevant, which is exactly the error seen. Hold out part of the data to measure recall and NDCG before and after. The whole corpus then has to be re-embedded. A fine-tuned reranker is a cheaper first step.",
    "qa-semantic-cache-risk": "Serving one tenant's cached answer to another tenant. A semantic cache embeds each incoming query and reuses a stored answer when a previous query is similar enough. With a loose threshold, two questions that only look alike count as a hit, and in a multi-tenant app the stored answer may contain another customer's private data. That is a data breach, not just a wrong answer. The fixes are to partition the cache by tenant and by permission scope so a lookup can never cross the boundary, tighten the threshold, include the document version in the key, and keep personalised answers out of the cache altogether.",
    "qa-embedding-mismatch": "The index now holds vectors from two different models. Each embedding model defines its own vector space, so a query embedded with the new model can only be compared meaningfully with documents embedded by the same model. Documents indexed before the switch still carry old vectors, and their similarity to new queries is close to random, which is why only they got worse. It looks like a model-quality problem but is really a migration problem. The fix is to re-embed the whole corpus into a new index, compare it with the old one on an evaluation set, then switch traffic over, and to store the model name and version with every vector.",
    "rag-rerank-first": "The reranker, in nearly every case. It needs less labelled data, because cross-encoder training on query-passage pairs is more data-efficient than contrastive training of a bi-encoder. It also requires no re-indexing when it changes: it only rescores a candidate set that has already been retrieved, so you can iterate on it daily and roll back in seconds. An embedding fine-tune forces a full re-embedding of the corpus before you can even evaluate the change in production, which is a far slower and more expensive feedback loop. The exception is when the relevant document is not in the candidate set at all, because no reranker can fix missing recall.",
    "qa-who-executes-tools": "The calling application does, never the model. The model sees the tool descriptions and, when a task needs outside information or an action, emits a structured request: a function name and arguments, usually as JSON. Your code parses that request, validates the arguments, decides whether to allow it, runs the function, and sends the result back as a new message, after which the model continues. This split is what makes tool use controllable. The application can check permissions, require human confirmation for risky actions, set timeouts and log every call. The model's output should be treated as an untrusted suggestion, not a command.",
    "qa-plan-vs-react": "Plan-and-execute. ReAct decides one action at a time, with a model call that re-reads the growing history before every step, so twenty steps cost twenty expensive calls over an ever longer context. When the order is clear upfront, one planning call can lay out all twenty steps, and cheaper models or plain code can carry them out, some in parallel, with the planner called again only if a step fails or returns something unexpected. The plan also gives you something to inspect and approve before anything runs. ReAct is the better fit when each step depends on what the previous one found.",
    "qa-topology-debug": "Hierarchical: one orchestrator delegates to sub-agents and combines their results. Every instruction and every result passes through a single point, so there is one trace to read and a clear owner for each decision, and you can replay a sub-agent with the exact input it was given. Peer-to-peer, where agents talk directly as equals, is more flexible but conversations can loop and it is hard to say which exchange caused a failure. A blackboard, where agents read and write shared state, suits an open set of contributors, but tracing who wrote what, and when, is harder. The orchestrator can become a bottleneck, which is the price of that clarity.",
    "qa-lost-in-middle": "Lost in the middle. Models recall information at the start and end of a long context more reliably than information in the middle, giving a U-shaped accuracy curve over position. It is a positional effect, which makes it different from context rot, the general decline in recall as the total number of tokens grows wherever the fact sits. The practical responses are to keep contexts short by retrieving and reranking, to place the most important material at the beginning or the end, and to repeat the key instructions or the question after long documents. Newer models show the effect less strongly, but it has not gone away.",
    "qa-subagents": "Sub-agents with their own context. The parent hands a focused sub-task to a sub-agent that starts with a clean context, does its searching and reading there, and returns only a condensed summary, often 1,000 to 2,000 tokens, with the findings and their sources. The full working trace stays out of the parent's context, so later steps are not degraded by thousands of tokens of intermediate tool output. Independent sub-tasks can also run in parallel. The costs are more total tokens and a summary that may drop a detail the parent needed, so the brief given to the sub-agent should say exactly what to return.",
    "cml-drift-no-labels": "Monitor the things you can see now. Track input feature distributions against a training or recent baseline with the population stability index or KS tests, the distribution of the model's predictions and scores, and data-quality signals such as the share of missing or out-of-range values and the volume by segment. A sudden shift usually points to a pipeline or upstream change, and a slow one to real change in behaviour. Drift does not always hurt performance, so treat it as a signal to investigate, not proof. Faster proxy labels, or manually labelling a small sample, shorten the wait. When true labels arrive, compute the real metrics and keep both views.",
    "cml-ab-peeking": "Peeking inflates the false-positive rate. A p-value's 5% guarantee assumes the sample size was fixed in advance and the data examined once. Checking every day and stopping at the first significant result gives the test many chances to cross the line by chance, and the real false-positive rate can be several times 5%. The effect size measured at that moment is also exaggerated. Either compute the required sample size from a power analysis and look once at the end, or use a method built for monitoring, such as group-sequential boundaries with alpha spending, or always-valid sequential p-values. Run at least a full weekly cycle so weekday effects average out.",
    "qa-rag-injection": "Limiting what the system is able to do, not asking the model to behave. Retrieved chunks are fed into the context beside the instructions, so an attacker who can get a document into the index can plant text the model may follow. No prompt wording removes that risk completely. The defences that hold are structural: give the RAG agent the fewest tools and permissions it needs, require separate confirmation or a human for actions such as sending email or exporting data, and block outbound channels it has no reason to use. Marking retrieved text as data, scanning documents at ingestion and filtering outputs all help, but they reduce the odds and do not bound the damage.",
}
